  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 223 PM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThursday - Friday Night...\n\nThe upper ridge will center over the lower Mississippi River \nValley/Gulf Coast states for the late week time frame, with the Ohio \nRiver Valley sitting on the NE periphery of this feature. NW flow \naloft will be in place over our area, sandwiched between the upper \nridge and a NE CONUS/Eastern Canada trough. This setup will put us \nin position for continued MCS potential as we get the overlap \nbetween strong instability and notable deep layer shear for this \ntime of year. \n\nIn addition to a potential severe weather threat (mainly damaging \nwind), any repeated rounds of MCS activity could create or \nexacerbate flooding concerns, depending on what happens through mid \nweek. \n\nHeat and humidity could also continue to be a concern as \ntemperatures could warm into the low and mid 90s in conjunction with \nlow and mid 70s dew points. This will remain low confidence however, \ngiven the potential for MCS activity to wreck a temp forecast \nThursday or Friday. Will handle any needed Heat Advisory headlines a \nday at a time in this pattern. Most likely areas for any headlines \nwould be across our SW CWA, closes to the center of the upper ridge.\n\n\nSaturday - Sunday...\n\nThe upper ridge strengthens to around 600 dam at H5 through the \nweekend, and also centers more over the Ozarks region by this time. \nThis will put our area more solidly under the influence of the \nsubsident airmass associated with an upper ridge, and also slightly \nmore intense heat. In fact, low and mid 90s from late week could \ngive way to mid and upper 90s by Sunday. Some heat headlines look to \nbe a good bet for at least our western or southwestern half of the \nCWA, if not most of the CWA. \n\nThe good news is that this should give us some reprieve from any MCS \nactivity as that threat would shift to the north and east thanks to \nthe movement of the upper ridge.\n\nMonday - Tuesday...\n\nThe upper ridge should retrograde back toward the Red River Valley \nby early next week, with NW flow aloft taking back hold of our area \nas the NE periphery of the upper ridge retreats. This will allow a \nweak frontal boundary trailing from a New England system to drop \ninto our region. Some mainly diurnally driven convection could occur \nwith this weak frontal passage on Monday, however we will likely be \npost-frontal on Tuesday with high pressure building in behind it. \nLikely a dry forecast for Tuesday unless the front gets hung up. \nPretty good agreement right now that it makes it through so will \nlean toward dry and overall cooler temperatures by Tuesday.",
